      "Freedom of expression is protected in many constitutions and in Article 19 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Yet every legal system limits some speech: fraud, perjury, true threats and incitement to imminent violence are restricted almost everywhere. The live question is not whether speech has limits but where they should fall and who should draw them.",
      "John Stuart Mill's On Liberty (1859) gives the classic defense. If a silenced opinion is true, we lose the truth; if it is false, we lose the clearer understanding of the truth that comes from its collision with error; and most often it holds part of the truth. Mill's harm principle says that power may rightly be exercised over a person against their will only to prevent harm to others. A later image, the 'marketplace of ideas', suggests that open competition among views tends to sort good from bad.",
      "Critics answer that the marketplace is not fair. Speakers with money, platforms or majority status are heard; others are drowned out or intimidated into silence. Some argue that hate speech harms its targets by undermining their standing as equal citizens, so a law against it protects rather than reduces the freedom of the group it shields. Many democracies, including Germany and Canada, restrict hate speech or Holocaust denial on such grounds.",
      "The United States takes a more protective line. In Brandenburg v. Ohio (1969) the Supreme Court held that advocacy of force may be punished only when it is directed to inciting imminent lawless action and is likely to produce it. Defenders say this bright line keeps governments from punishing unpopular dissent, which they have often done; critics say it leaves real harms unaddressed.",
      "A further dispute concerns who limits speech. Constitutional guarantees usually bind governments, not private companies, employers or universities. Some argue that when a few platforms host most public debate, their moderation decisions matter as much as laws; others reply that forcing private parties to carry speech violates their own freedom.",
      "Underneath lies a choice about what speech is for: discovering truth, enabling self-government, expressing individual autonomy, or some mix. Each rationale draws the line in a different place, and the hardest cases are those where two of them pull apart."
