      "In 1890 Samuel Warren and Louis Brandeis argued in the Harvard Law Review for a 'right to be let alone', prompted by new portable cameras and an intrusive press. Since then the meaning of privacy has been disputed: is it control over information about oneself, protection of a personal space, freedom from being watched, or a condition for forming one's own thoughts?",
      "Governments justify surveillance by security. Interception of communications, data retention and camera networks can help prevent terrorism and solve crimes. In 2013 documents disclosed by Edward Snowden showed that the US National Security Agency had collected bulk records of domestic telephone calls; a federal appeals court later held that program was not authorized by the statute relied on, and Congress replaced it in 2015.",
      "A common argument says 'if you have nothing to hide, you have nothing to fear.' Critics reply that everyone has lawful things they reasonably keep private, that information can be misused by later governments, and that being watched changes behavior: people who know they are observed may avoid lawful but unpopular associations or reading. This is often called a chilling effect. Jeremy Bentham's design for a panopticon prison, in which inmates could always be watched but never know when, is a common image of that effect.",
      "Much collection is now done by companies. Firms gather data about browsing, location and purchases to target advertising, and people agree to it by accepting terms they rarely read. Some argue this consent is real and the services are a fair trade; others argue it is not meaningful consent, and that aggregated data can reveal far more than any single item.",
      "Legal responses differ. The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (2018) requires a lawful basis for processing personal data and gives individuals rights of access and erasure. Other systems rely more on sector-by-sector rules and contract. Defenders of lighter regulation stress innovation and free services; defenders of stronger rules stress power imbalances between individuals and data holders.",
      "Underlying these debates is the question whether privacy is mainly an individual good that each person may trade away, or also a collective good that a free society needs whatever individuals choose."
