      "Capital punishment is execution by the state as punishment for a crime. Most countries have abolished it in law or practice, while others, including the United States, China, Iran and Saudi Arabia, still carry out executions. The debate draws on general theories of why the state may punish at all.",
      "Retributivists hold that punishment is justified because offenders deserve it, in proportion to their wrongdoing. Kant argued that a murderer must die, and that even a society about to dissolve itself should first execute the last murderer in prison. Other retributivists accept desert but deny it requires death: proportion, they say, sets an upper limit or a ranking of punishments, not an exact copy of the crime.",
      "Consequentialists justify punishment by its effects, chiefly deterrence and incapacitation. Whether the death penalty deters murder better than long imprisonment is disputed. A 2012 report of the US National Research Council concluded that existing studies were not informative about the deterrent effect of capital punishment and should not be used to guide policy.",
      "Opponents stress error. Courts make mistakes, and execution cannot be undone. In the United States more than 190 people sentenced to death have later been exonerated since 1973, according to the Death Penalty Information Center. Opponents also point to disparities by race of victim, by region and by quality of defense counsel, arguing that the penalty cannot be administered fairly.",
      "Some argue that execution violates human dignity or a right to life that even a murderer keeps; Cesare Beccaria made an early case against it in 1764. Supporters reply that taking a murderer's life honors the dignity of the victim and treats the offender as a responsible agent, and that some crimes are so grave that any lesser penalty fails to express their seriousness.",
      "The debate thus combines empirical questions about deterrence and error with moral questions about desert, rights and what a state may do in its citizens' name."
