      "The phrase 'nature and nurture' was popularized by Francis Galton in the nineteenth century. The question it names is how far human traits such as intelligence, personality and mental illness are shaped by inherited biology and how far by environment, upbringing and culture.",
      "Twin and adoption studies are the main evidence. Identical twins share essentially all their genes and fraternal twins about half of their varying genes; if identical twins are more alike on a trait, that suggests a genetic contribution. Such studies estimate heritability: the proportion of variation in a trait within a particular population that is associated with genetic variation. Many psychological traits show moderate heritability.",
      "Heritability is often misread. It describes variation in a population, not how much of an individual's trait comes from genes. It can change when the environment changes: if everyone gets the same good schooling, more of the remaining variation will track genes. And a highly heritable trait can still be changed by the environment; height is highly heritable, yet average heights rose sharply over the twentieth century with better nutrition.",
      "Critics of twin studies question the assumption that identical and fraternal twins share equally similar environments, and note that heritability figures from one population do not transfer to another. Others stress that genes and environment interact: a genetic tendency may be expressed only in certain environments, and people with different temperaments choose and evoke different environments.",
      "The debate has political weight. Some fear that emphasizing genes encourages fatalism or justifies inequality, as eugenics movements did in the early twentieth century. Others reply that denying biology leads to blaming parents or individuals for differences they did not cause, and that knowing a risk is inherited can guide help.",
      "Many researchers now say the dichotomy is false, because traits develop through constant interaction. Others answer that it is still meaningful to ask how much of the variation among people is linked to genetic versus environmental differences."
